WEBVTT

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.252
The Atacama Desert in northern Chile is famed for its dark night skies,

00:00:09.252 --> 00:00:15.040
which can be enjoyed in their full glory thanks to the absence of light pollution.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.550
But even the darkest sky is not completely dark.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.428
Astronomers at ESO's observatories often encounter

00:00:24.428 --> 00:00:28.256
a natural light phenomenon above ESO's telescopes,

00:00:28.256 --> 00:00:30.400
known as the zodiacal light.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.863
This is the ESOcast! Cutting-edge science and life behind the scenes of ESO,

00:00:41.863 --> 00:00:48.326
the European Southern Observatory. Exploring the ultimate frontier with our host,

00:00:48.326 --> 00:00:50.400
Dr J, a.k.a. Dr Joe Liske.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.400
Hello and welcome to another episode of the ESOcast.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.537
Now, ESO's observatories are such incredibly dark sites that normally,

00:00:59.537 --> 00:01:02.940
the only thing that illuminates them on a moonless night is

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.400
the faint light from the billions of stars in the Milky Way.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.494
But there are a number of other interesting phenomena

00:01:09.494 --> 00:01:12.290
that can be observed in the skies above the telescopes.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.989
These include, for example,

00:01:13.989 --> 00:01:17.347
the faint veil of airglow and the occasional appearance

00:01:17.347 --> 00:01:20.400
of red sprites above the odd distant thunderstorm.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.800
And then, every night, especially in the hours just after dusk or before dawn, this faint,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.400
fuzzy column of light appears in the sky, just above the horizon, extending upwards.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:40.400
This ghostly glow is known as zodiacal light.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.877
Shortly after sunset, just as stars begin to appear in the sky,

00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:50.400
the first hints of zodiacal light also become visible.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.777
As darkness sweeps over the desert,

00:01:54.777 --> 00:01:58.517
this light becomes more prominent and can be seen as

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:02.400
a bright column of light reaching up from the horizon.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.821
This luminous column follows the starry background across the sky,

00:02:07.821 --> 00:02:13.890
eventually disappearing below the horizon as the Earth rotates on its axis.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:19.335
Even after the brightest part of the zodiacal light has dropped below the horizon,

00:02:19.335 --> 00:02:21.374
traces of it are still present,

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:24.334
although it now resembles an extremely faint,

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:29.400
wispy bridge that brightens again in the early morning, just before daybreak.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.930
The origins of zodiacal light are to be found in the inner solar system.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:44.090
The sun is surrounded by tiny grains of ice and dust that are constantly

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:49.690
being replenished by crumbling icy comets and colliding asteroids.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:58.620
These grains are distributed within the same flat disk of space inhabited by the planets.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:08.235
When viewed from Earth, this disk appears as a narrow path across the sky, called the ecliptic,

00:03:08.235 --> 00:03:15.510
which the Sun, Moon and planets all appear to follow as they move in the sky.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:15.811
Now,

00:03:15.811 --> 00:03:20.134
zodiacal light is created when light coming from the Sun is scattered

00:03:20.134 --> 00:03:24.270
forwards off the particles in this disk in the direction of Earth.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.196
Now when viewed from Earth,

00:03:26.196 --> 00:03:30.853
this creates the appearance of a continuous band of light along the

00:03:30.853 --> 00:03:35.370
ecliptic that gets fainter as you look further away from the Sun.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:41.410
Now the constellations of the zodiac of course also lie along the

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:47.400
ecliptic and that's why we call this ghostly glow zodiacal light.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.045
Along the ecliptic, high in the sky,

00:03:53.045 --> 00:04:00.250
an oval patch of illumination can also appear, known as gegenschein or counterglow.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.197
Named by the German explorer Alexander von Humboldt,

00:04:04.197 --> 00:04:07.644
this phenomenon is created by sunlight that is

00:04:07.644 --> 00:04:11.690
scattered backwards off interplanetary dust particles.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:17.170
It can be seen at the point in the sky opposite the sun.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.570
A similar phenomenon can be experienced here on Earth.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:25.947
When you turn your back to the sun in foggy weather,

00:04:25.947 --> 00:04:32.770
a halo of light called a glory sometimes appears around your shadow on the ground.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:38.851
The zodiacal light phenomenon seems to have been first investigated in the late 1600s by the

00:04:38.851 --> 00:04:44.750
Italian astronomer Giovanni Cassini and the Swiss mathematician Nicolas Fatio de Duillier.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.850
They were absolutely fascinated with this light in the sky.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.090
And of course, back in the 17th century, there was very little light pollution.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.400
And so for them, it was relatively easy to observe this phenomenon, even from cities.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.355
Modern observations have shown that the Solar System

00:05:05.355 --> 00:05:09.330
might not be the only one to exhibit zodiacal light.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:15.587
Data from the Very Large Telescope Interferometer at ESO's Paranal Observatory has

00:05:15.587 --> 00:05:22.400
revealed that numerous other planetary systems are also surrounded by interplanetary dust.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.759
So zodiacal light is a really photogenic feature,

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:33.371
and so it's no surprise that it's become a popular

00:05:33.371 --> 00:05:36.400
subject for nighttime photographers in the Atacama Desert.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:41.669
The fact that it's so prominent at ESO's observatories is a beautiful demonstration

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:46.000
of how incredibly good the observing conditions at these sites actually are.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.270
This is Dr J, signing off for the ESOcast.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.500
Join me again next time for another cosmic adventure.

